<div class="ltx_page_main">
<div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Hodge structures associated to <math id="m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SU(p,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SU(p,1)</annotation></semantics></math></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Salman Abdulali
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span>Department of Mathematics, East Carolina University, Greenville, NC 27858, USA
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:abdulalis@ecu.edu">abdulalis@ecu.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be an abelian variety over <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the semisimple part of the Hodge group of <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a product of copies of <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SU(p,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SU(p,1)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.
We show that any effective Tate twist of a Hodge structure occurring in the cohomology of <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to a Hodge structure in the cohomology of some abelian variety.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Key words and phrases: </h6>Hodge structure, abelian variety, Kuga fiber variety, Tate twist
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">2010 Mathematics Subject Classification</h6>Primary 14C30, 14K20
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper is a contribution to the classification of Hodge structures which occur in the cohomology of abelian varieties.
We consider the question of when an effective Tate twist of such a Hodge structure is itself isomorphic to a Hodge structure in the cohomology of some abelian variety.
Our interest in this question arises from the fact that the general Hodge conjecture, as formulated by Grothendieck, implies that any effective Tate twist of the Hodge structure occurs in the cohomology of some smooth, projective algebraic (not necessarily abelian) variety <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that a (rational) Hodge structure of weight <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite dimensional vector space <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, together with a decomposition</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbb{C}}=\bigoplus_{p+q=n}V^{p,q}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbb{C}}=\bigoplus_{p+q=n}V^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">such that <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{q,p}=\overline{V^{p,q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{q,p}=\overline{V^{p,q}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The Hodge structure is called <em id="S1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">effective</em> if <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{p,q}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{p,q}=0</annotation></semantics></math> unless <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <em id="S1.p2.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Tate twist</em> <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(m)</annotation></semantics></math> is the Hodge structure of weight <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-2m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-2m</annotation></semantics></math> given by
<math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(m)^{p,q}=V^{p+m,q+m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(m)^{p,q}=V^{p+m,q+m}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In a series of papers
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>
we have shown for a large class of abelian varieties that every effective Tate twist of a Hodge
structure contained in the cohomology of one of these abelian varieties is isomorphic to
a Hodge structure occurring in the cohomology of some abelian variety.
Our earlier results apply to abelian varieties of type IV in only a few cases—namely,
when the Hodge group is semisimple <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, or when
the abelian variety is of CM-type <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, or, when the semisimple part
of the Hodge group is a product of groups of the form <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SU(p+1,p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SU(p+1,p)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.
In this paper we extend these results to any abelian variety <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> such that the semisimple part of
the Hodge group of <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a product of copies of <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SU(p,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SU(p,1)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>;
see Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 10. ‣ 4. Abelian varieties associated to 
        
          
            ⁢
            S
            U
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                (
                p
                ,
                1
                ) ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> for the precise statement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">That not all abelian varieties have the above property was shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>*Theorem 5.5, p. 926 we showed the existence of a Hodge structure <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> which occurs in
the cohomology of an abelian variety, such that <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(1)</annotation></semantics></math> is effective but does not occur in
the cohomology of <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">any</em> abelian variety.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We draw attention to two new features of this paper.
First, the concept of <em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">semidomination</em> (Definition <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Definition 4. ‣ 3. Semidomination ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>)
generalizes the concept of <em id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">weak self-domination</em> introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>,
and allows us to work with the semisimple part of the Hodge group of an abelian variety
whose Hodge group is neither semisimple nor commutative (see Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ 3. Semidomination ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).
Second, families of abelian varieties which are <em id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> of <span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">pel</span>-type play a critical role,
even though our main concern is a general member of a <span id="S1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">pel</span>-family.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmackowledgementsx1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ackowledgements">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmackowledgementsx1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ackowledgements</span></span><span id="Thmackowledgementsx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmackowledgementsx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmackowledgementsx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">I am grateful to the referees for detailed and insightful comments which have led to significant improvements to this paper.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmnotationsx1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_notations">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmnotationsx1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Notations and conventions</span></span><span id="Thmnotationsx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmnotationsx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmnotationsx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All representations are finite-dimensional and algebraic.
The derived group of a group <math id="Thmnotationsx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="Thmnotationsx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
All abelian varieties are over <math id="Thmnotationsx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For an abelian variety <math id="Thmnotationsx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, we let</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(A)=\End(A)\otimes\mathbb{Q}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(A)=\End(A)\otimes\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmnotationsx1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">be its endomorphism algebra, <math id="Thmnotationsx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(A)</annotation></semantics></math> its Lefschetz group, <math id="Thmnotationsx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A)</annotation></semantics></math> its Hodge group,
<math id="Thmnotationsx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> the derived group of <math id="Thmnotationsx1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(A)</annotation></semantics></math>, and, <math id="Thmnotationsx1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> the derived group of <math id="Thmnotationsx1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a finite field extension <math id="Thmnotationsx1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> of a field <math id="Thmnotationsx1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, we let <math id="Thmnotationsx1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Res_{E/F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Res</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Res_{E/F}</annotation></semantics></math> be
the restriction of scalars functor, from varieties over <math id="Thmnotationsx1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> to varieties over <math id="Thmnotationsx1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.
The center of a group <math id="Thmnotationsx1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="Thmnotationsx1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a topological group <math id="Thmnotationsx1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by <math id="Thmnotationsx1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> the connected component of the identity.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Kuga fiber varieties</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We briefly recall Kuga’s construction of families of abelian varieties <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>;
full details may be found in Satake’s book <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected, semisimple, linear algebraic group over <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> with identity <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume that <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is of hermitian type, and has no nontrivial, connected, normal <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-subgroup
<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math> compact.
Then <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=G(\mathbb{R})^{0}/K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=G(\mathbb{R})^{0}/K</annotation></semantics></math> is a bounded symmetric domain for
a maximal compact subgroup <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\mathbb{R})^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\mathbb{R})^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{g}=\Lie G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔤</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Lie</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{g}=\Lie G</annotation></semantics></math> be the Lie algebra of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{k}=\Lie K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔨</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Lie</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{k}=\Lie K</annotation></semantics></math>, and let
<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{g}_{\mathbb{R}}=\mathfrak{k}\oplus\mathfrak{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔤</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔨</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>𝔭</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{g}_{\mathbb{R}}=\mathfrak{k}\oplus\mathfrak{p}</annotation></semantics></math> be the corresponding Cartan decomposition.
Differentiating the natural map <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu\colon G(\mathbb{R})^{0}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu\colon G(\mathbb{R})^{0}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> induces an isomorphism of
<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔭</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{p}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{o}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{o}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, the tangent space of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o=\nu(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o=\nu(1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and there exists
a unique <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}\in Z(\mathfrak{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔨</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}\in Z(\mathfrak{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, called the <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S2.SS1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-element</em> at <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math>,
such that <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ad H_{0}|\mathfrak{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ad</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝔭</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ad H_{0}|\mathfrak{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is the complex structure on <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{o}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{o}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> be a nondegenerate alternating form on a finite-dimensional rational vector space <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>.
The symplectic group <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sp(V,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sp(V,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebraic group of hermitian type;
the associated symmetric domain is the <em id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Siegel space</em></p>
<table id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}(V,\beta)=\{\,J\in GL(V_{\mathbb{R}})\mid J^{2}=-I\text{ and }\\
\beta(x,Jy)\text{ is symmetric, positive definite}\,\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>𝔖</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>J</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>I</mi><mtext> and </mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext> is symmetric, positive definite</mtext><mo lspace="0.340em" stretchy="false">}</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}(V,\beta)=\{\,J\in GL(V_{\mathbb{R}})\mid J^{2}=-I\text{ and }\\
\beta(x,Jy)\text{ is symmetric, positive definite}\,\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sp(V,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sp(V,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}(V,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}(V,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> by conjugation.
The <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-element at <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\in\mathfrak{S}(V,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\in\mathfrak{S}(V,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J/2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\colon G\to Sp(V,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\colon G\to Sp(V,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> be a representation defined over <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We say that <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the <em id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-condition</em> relative to the <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-elements
<math id="S2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}_{0}=J/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}_{0}=J/2</annotation></semantics></math> if</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[d\rho(H_{0})-H^{\prime}_{0},d\rho(g)]=0\qquad\text{for all }g\in\mathfrak{g}_{\mathbb{R}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔤</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[d\rho(H_{0})-H^{\prime}_{0},d\rho(g)]=0\qquad\text{for all }g\in\mathfrak{g}_{\mathbb{R}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">If this is satisfied, then there exists a unique holomorphic map
<math id="S2.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\colon X\to\mathfrak{S}(V,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝔖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\colon X\to\mathfrak{S}(V,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(o)=J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(o)=J</annotation></semantics></math>, and the pair <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\rho,\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\rho,\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>
is equivariant in the sense that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(g\cdot x)=\rho(g)\cdot\tau(x)\qquad\text{for all }g\in G(\mathbb{R})^{0},\ x\in X." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(g\cdot x)=\rho(g)\cdot\tau(x)\qquad\text{for all }g\in G(\mathbb{R})^{0},\ x\in X.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> be a torsion-free arithmetic subgroup of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> a lattice in <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\Gamma)L=L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\Gamma)L=L</annotation></semantics></math>.
The natural map</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}=(\Gamma\ltimes_{\rho}L)\backslash(X\times V_{\mathbb{R}})\longrightarrow\mathcal{V}=\Gamma\backslash X" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋉</mo><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=(\Gamma\ltimes_{\rho}L)\backslash(X\times V_{\mathbb{R}})\longrightarrow\mathcal{V}=\Gamma\backslash X</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.4" class="ltx_p">is a morphism of smooth quasiprojective algebraic varieties
(Borel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>*Theorem 3.10, p. 559 and Deligne <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>*p. 74),
so that <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is a fiber variety over <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> called a <em id="S2.SS1.p4.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Kuga fiber variety</em>.
The fiber <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> over any point <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is an abelian variety isomorphic to the torus
<math id="S2.SS1.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbb{R}}/L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbb{R}}/L</annotation></semantics></math> with the complex structure <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is a point in <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> lying over <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Two algebraic groups</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We recall two algebraic groups associated to an abelian variety <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=H_{1}(A,\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=H_{1}(A,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> be an alternating Riemann form for <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Hodge group</em> <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Lefschetz group</em> <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(A)</annotation></semantics></math> are
reductive <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-subgroups of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The Hodge group (or <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">special Mumford-Tate group</em>) is characterized by the property that
for any positive integer <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, the invariants of the action of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(A^{k},\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(A^{k},\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>
form the ring <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(A^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(A^{k})</annotation></semantics></math> of Hodge classes (Mumford <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>).
The Lefschetz group is defined to be the centralizer of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\End(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\End(A)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sp(V,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sp(V,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>;
it is characterized by the property that for any positive integer <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>,
the subring of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(A^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(A^{k})</annotation></semantics></math> generated by the classes of divisors equals
<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(A^{k},\mathbb{C})^{L(A)_{\mathbb{C}}}\cap H^{\bullet}(A^{k},\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(A^{k},\mathbb{C})^{L(A)_{\mathbb{C}}}\cap H^{\bullet}(A^{k},\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>
(Milne <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>*Theorem 3.2, p. 656 and Murty <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>*§3.6.2, p. 93).
Clearly,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A)\subset L(A)\subset Sp(V,\beta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A)\subset L(A)\subset Sp(V,\beta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The inclusion <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A)\hookrightarrow Sp(V,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A)\hookrightarrow Sp(V,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-condition (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1. Kuga fiber varieties ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>)
with respect to suitable <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-elements.
Taking <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=H_{1}(A,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=H_{1}(A,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> to be any torsion-free arithmetic subgroup of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(x)\in L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(x)\in L</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in L</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain a
Kuga fiber variety having <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> as a fiber; this is called the <em id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Hodge family</em> determined by
<math id="S2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> (see Mumford <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The inclusion <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}(A)^{0}\hookrightarrow Sp(V,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}(A)^{0}\hookrightarrow Sp(V,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-condition,
and hence may be used to define a Kuga fiber variety having <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> as a fiber.
These families of abelian varieties are generalizations of the <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">pel</span>-families constructed by
Shimura in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be an abelian variety such that <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math> has no compact factors.
If <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A)=G_{1}\times G_{2}\times\dots\times G_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A)=G_{1}\times G_{2}\times\dots\times G_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is isogenous to a product</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\sim A_{1}\times A_{2}\times\dots\times A_{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\sim A_{1}\times A_{2}\times\dots\times A_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A_{i})=G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A_{i})=G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq t</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Hodge family of <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> be such that <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\mathcal{A}_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\mathcal{A}_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\colon G(A)\to Sp(V,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\colon G(A)\to Sp(V,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-representation defining <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume without loss of generality that <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}=\mathcal{V}_{1}\times\dots\times\mathcal{V}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}=\mathcal{V}_{1}\times\dots\times\mathcal{V}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is an arithmetic variety associated to <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=(P_{1},\dots,P_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=(P_{1},\dots,P_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We recall that a representation of <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is called <em id="S2.SS2.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">primary</em> if all irreducible subrepresentations are equivalent.
Write <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\bigoplus_{j=1}^{s}V_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\bigoplus_{j=1}^{s}V_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> where the <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are the maximal primary <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-submodules of <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>,
and let</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{j}\colon G\to Sp(V_{j},\beta|V_{j}\times V_{j})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{j}\colon G\to Sp(V_{j},\beta|V_{j}\times V_{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">be the restriction of <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then each <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-condition (see Satake <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>*Lemma IV.4.2, p. 181, and the remarks following it).
By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>*Lemma 2.4.1, p. 919, each <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is nontrivial on <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for at most one <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.
We may therefore write <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\bigoplus_{i=1}^{t}\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\bigoplus_{i=1}^{t}\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a representation of <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-condition.
Let <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{i}\to\mathcal{V}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{i}\to\mathcal{V}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Kuga fiber variety determined by <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the fiber of <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A_{i})=G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A_{i})=G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is isogenous to <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1}\times A_{2}\times\dots\times A_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1}\times A_{2}\times\dots\times A_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any abelian variety <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, the center of <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A)</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in the center of <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(A)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=H_{1}(A,\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=H_{1}(A,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> be an alternating Riemann form for <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, and,
let <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> be the center of <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(A)</annotation></semantics></math> is the centralizer of <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\End(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\End(V)</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subset D(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subset D(A)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(A)</annotation></semantics></math> is (by definition) the centralizer of <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(A)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sp(V,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sp(V,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>, we see that
<math id="S2.SS2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is in the center of <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(A)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> are abelian varieties, then</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}(A\times B,\mathbb{Q})=H_{1}(A,\mathbb{Q})\oplus H_{1}(B,\mathbb{Q})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}(A\times B,\mathbb{Q})=H_{1}(A,\mathbb{Q})\oplus H_{1}(B,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">as Hodge structures,
so <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A\times B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A\times B)</annotation></semantics></math> acts on the (co)homology of each factor.
It follows that <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A\times B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A\times B)</annotation></semantics></math> is a subgroup of <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A)\times G(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A)\times G(B)</annotation></semantics></math> whose projection to each factor is surjective (see Imai <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Semidomination</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We say that a Hodge structure <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbb{C}}=\bigoplus_{p+q=n}V^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbb{C}}=\bigoplus_{p+q=n}V^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is
<em id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fully twisted</em> if it is effective, and, <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{n,0}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{n,0}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
We say that a smooth, projective algebraic variety <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dominated</em> by
a set <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> of smooth, projective algebraic varieties over <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> if, for any irreducible Hodge
structure <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> in the cohomology of <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a fully twisted Hodge structure <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
in the cohomology of some <math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to a Tate twist of <math id="S3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>.
Grothendieck <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>*p. 301 observed that if <math id="S3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is dominated by <math id="S3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the usual Hodge conjecture for <math id="S3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\times X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\times X</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, implies the general Hodge conjecture for <math id="S3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We now introduce the concept of <em id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">semidomination</em> of an abelian variety, which will help us to prove that certain abelian varieties are dominated by classes of abelian varieties. Its main utility is in allowing us to work with the semisimple part of the Hodge group of an abelian variety whose Hodge group is neither semisimple nor commutative.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We say that an abelian variety <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">semidominated</em> by a set <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> of abelian varieties if,
given any nontrivial irreducible representation <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A)_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A)_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> occurs in
<math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}(A,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}(A,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\rho}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\rho}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, a positive integer <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math>, and,
<math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\rho}\subset H^{c_{\rho}}(A_{\rho},\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\rho}\subset H^{c_{\rho}}(A_{\rho},\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>, such that</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A_{\rho})_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A_{\rho})_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>-submodule of <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{c_{\rho}}(A_{\rho},\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{c_{\rho}}(A_{\rho},\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the action of <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A\times A_{\rho})_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A\times A_{\rho})_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to
<math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\circ p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\circ p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A)\times G^{\prime}(A_{\rho})\supset G^{\prime}(A\times A_{\rho})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle p_{1}}}{{\longrightarrow}}G^{\prime}(A)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mover><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A)\times G^{\prime}(A_{\rho})\supset G^{\prime}(A\times A_{\rho})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle p_{1}}}{{\longrightarrow}}G^{\prime}(A)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is the projection to the first factor (see Remark <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Remark 3. ‣ 2.2. Two algebraic groups ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>),</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">for each <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\Aut(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\Aut(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>, the Galois conjugate <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(V_{\rho})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(V_{\rho})</annotation></semantics></math>
contains a nonzero <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c_{\rho},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c_{\rho},0)</annotation></semantics></math>-form.
(The action of <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{c_{\rho}}(A_{\rho},\mathbb{C})=H^{c_{\rho}}(A_{\rho},\mathbb{Q})\otimes\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{c_{\rho}}(A_{\rho},\mathbb{C})=H^{c_{\rho}}(A_{\rho},\mathbb{Q})\otimes\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>
is through the second factor.)</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 5</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will see in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ 3. Semidomination ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> below that if <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is semidominated by <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is dominated by abelian varieties.
However, an abelian variety may be dominated by abelian varieties without being semidominated
by any set of abelian varieties.
For example, let <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be a simple abelian variety of type III which satisfies the hypotheses of
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>*Theorem 4.1, p. 922, and assume further that <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(A)</annotation></semantics></math> has center <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\dim_{D(A)}H^{1}(A,\mathbb{Q})\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\dim_{D(A)}H^{1}(A,\mathbb{Q})\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is dominated by the set of powers of itself <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>*Theorem 4.1, p. 922, but we claim that it is not semidominated by any set of abelian varieties.
Suppose otherwise.
Then by the arguments in the proof of
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>*Theorem 5.5, p. 926, <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is semidominated by the set of powers of <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now let <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\subset H^{m}(A,\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\subset H^{m}(A,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> be an irreducible Hodge structure containing the representation <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}=U_{1,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}=U_{1,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>*Theorem 4.1, p. 922.
Then <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is fully twisted.
Let <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}\subset H^{c}(A^{n},\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}\subset H^{c}(A^{n},\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A)_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A)_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>-module associated to <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by Definition <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Definition 4. ‣ 3. Semidomination ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>*Lemma 3.3.1, p. 921, we must have <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=m</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the smallest Hodge structure <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{V}</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> (see the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ 3. Semidomination ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).
This implies that every Galois conjugate of <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> contains an <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,0)</annotation></semantics></math>-form, which is seen to be false in the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>*Theorem 4.1, p. 922.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is semidominated by <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is semidominated by <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, and if
<math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A\times B)=G^{\prime}(A)\times G^{\prime}(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A\times B)=G^{\prime}(A)\times G^{\prime}(B)</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\times B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\times B</annotation></semantics></math> is semidominated by</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{X}\cdot\mathcal{Y}=\{X\times Y\mid X\in\mathcal{X},\ Y\in\mathcal{Y}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\cdot\mathcal{Y}=\{X\times Y\mid X\in\mathcal{X},\ Y\in\mathcal{Y}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Any irreducible representation of <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A\times B)_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A\times B)_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> is of the form <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\otimes\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\otimes\tau</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is an irreducible representation of <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A)_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A)_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is an irreducible representation of <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(B)_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(B)_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\rho\otimes\tau}=\begin{cases}V_{\rho}\otimes V_{\tau}&amp;\text{if both $\rho$ and $\tau$ are nontrivial;}\\
V_{\rho}&amp;\text{if $\rho$ is nontrivial but $\tau$ is trivial;}\\
V_{\tau}&amp;\text{if $\tau$ is nontrivial but $\rho$ is trivial.}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if both </mtext><mi>ρ</mi><mtext> and </mtext><mi>τ</mi><mtext> are nontrivial;</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mi>ρ</mi><mtext> is nontrivial but </mtext><mi>τ</mi><mtext> is trivial;</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mi>τ</mi><mtext> is nontrivial but </mtext><mi>ρ</mi><mtext> is trivial.</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\rho\otimes\tau}=\begin{cases}V_{\rho}\otimes V_{\tau}&amp;\text{if both $\rho$ and $\tau$ are nontrivial;}\\
V_{\rho}&amp;\text{if $\rho$ is nontrivial but $\tau$ is trivial;}\\
V_{\tau}&amp;\text{if $\tau$ is nontrivial but $\rho$ is trivial.}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Then each Galois conjugate of <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\rho\otimes\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\rho\otimes\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c_{\rho\otimes\tau},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c_{\rho\otimes\tau},0)</annotation></semantics></math>-form for some <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\rho\otimes\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\rho\otimes\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be an abelian variety semidominated by <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is dominated by the set of abelian varieties of the form <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\times C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\times C</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is of CM-type.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> be any irreducible Hodge structure in the cohomology of <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> acts trivially on <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is of CM-type, so by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>*Theorem 3, p. 159 there exists an abelian variety <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of CM-type,
and a fully twisted Hodge structure <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in the cohomology of <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>,
such that <math id="S3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to a Tate twist of <math id="S3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>.
Otherwise, let <math id="S3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be any irreducible <math id="S3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A)_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A)_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>-submodule of <math id="S3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>;
it is necessarily irreducible as a <math id="S3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A)_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A)_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>-module.
Since <math id="S3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is semidominated by <math id="S3.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists <math id="S3.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
an irreducible <math id="S3.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(B)_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(B)_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>-submodule <math id="S3.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{c_{\rho}}(B,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{c_{\rho}}(B,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>
satisfying the conditions of Definition <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Definition 4. ‣ 3. Semidomination ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be the torus <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A\times B)/G^{\prime}(A\times B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A\times B)/G^{\prime}(A\times B)</annotation></semantics></math>,
and let <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to GL(W_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to GL(W_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> be any faithful representation of <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since every representation of <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A\times B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A\times B)</annotation></semantics></math> occurs in the tensor algebra of
<math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(A\times B,\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(A\times B,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>*Proposition 3.1(a), p. 40, there exists
a Hodge structure <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> in the cohomology of some power of <math id="S3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\times B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\times B</annotation></semantics></math> such that
the action of <math id="S3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A\times B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A\times B)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to the representation
<math id="S3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A\times B)\to T\to GL(W_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A\times B)\to T\to GL(W_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(W)=T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(W)=T</annotation></semantics></math>, so, <math id="S3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is of CM-type.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text"> is equivalent to <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> as a <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A\times B)_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A\times B)_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>-module.
Therefore <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\rho}\otimes\chi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>χ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\rho}\otimes\chi</annotation></semantics></math> as a <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A\times B)_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A\times B)_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>-module,
where <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math> is a character of <math id="S3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The character <math id="S3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math> occurs in the tensor algebra of <math id="S3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\chi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>χ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\chi}</annotation></semantics></math> be its representation space, and let <math id="S3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> be an irreducible Hodge structure
in the tensor algebra of <math id="S3.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S3.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\chi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>χ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\chi}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the main theorem of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, there exists an abelian variety <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of CM-type and
an irreducible Hodge structure <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\prime}\subset H^{c}(C,\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\prime}\subset H^{c}(C,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to
a Tate twist <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(w)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, and, <math id="S3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is fully twisted.
Let <math id="S3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\colon Z(w)\to Z^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\colon Z(w)\to Z^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be an isomorphism of Hodge structures, and
let <math id="S3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\prime}_{\chi}=\varphi(W_{\chi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>χ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>χ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\prime}_{\chi}=\varphi(W_{\chi})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then there exists <math id="S3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\Aut(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\Aut(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(Z^{\prime}_{\chi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>χ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(Z^{\prime}_{\chi})</annotation></semantics></math> contains
a nonzero <math id="S3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,0)</annotation></semantics></math>-form.
Let</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}=V_{\rho}\otimes Z^{\prime}_{\chi}\subset H^{c_{\rho}+c}(B\times C,\mathbb{C})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>χ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}=V_{\rho}\otimes Z^{\prime}_{\chi}\subset H^{c_{\rho}+c}(B\times C,\mathbb{C}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{V}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{V}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the smallest Hodge substructure of <math id="S3.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{c_{\rho}+c}(B\times C,\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{c_{\rho}+c}(B\times C,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S3.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{V}^{\prime}_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{V}^{\prime}_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S3.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S3.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{V}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{V}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is fully twisted because <math id="S3.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(U^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(U^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> contains a nonzero <math id="S3.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c_{\rho}+c,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c_{\rho}+c,0)</annotation></semantics></math>-form.
Recall that <math id="S3.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is an irreducible <math id="S3.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A)</annotation></semantics></math>-module, and note that <math id="S3.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{V}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{V}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is
a primary <math id="S3.p7.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(B\times C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(B\times C)</annotation></semantics></math>-module, i.e., all of its irreducible submodules are mutually equivalent.
Let <math id="S3.p7.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=G(A\times B\times C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=G(A\times B\times C)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We have a <math id="S3.p7.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>-isomorphism between <math id="S3.p7.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p7.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>,
so <math id="S3.p7.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hom_{G_{\mathbb{C}}}(V_{\mathbb{C}},\widetilde{V}^{\prime}_{\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>hom</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hom_{G_{\mathbb{C}}}(V_{\mathbb{C}},\widetilde{V}^{\prime}_{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math> is nontrivial.
Since <math id="S3.p7.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is Zariski-dense in <math id="S3.p7.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> (Rosenlicht <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>*Corollary, p. 44),
we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hom_{G(\mathbb{C})}(V_{\mathbb{C}},\widetilde{V}^{\prime}_{\mathbb{C}})=\hom_{G(\mathbb{Q})}(V_{\mathbb{C}},\widetilde{V}^{\prime}_{\mathbb{C}})=\hom_{G}(V,\widetilde{V}^{\prime})\otimes\mathbb{C}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>hom</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>hom</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>hom</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hom_{G(\mathbb{C})}(V_{\mathbb{C}},\widetilde{V}^{\prime}_{\mathbb{C}})=\hom_{G(\mathbb{Q})}(V_{\mathbb{C}},\widetilde{V}^{\prime}_{\mathbb{C}})=\hom_{G}(V,\widetilde{V}^{\prime})\otimes\mathbb{C}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p7.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.3.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore <math id="S3.p7.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hom_{G}(V,\widetilde{V}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>hom</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hom_{G}(V,\widetilde{V}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is nontrivial, and <math id="S3.p7.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to a Tate twist of
an irreducible Hodge structure <math id="S3.p7.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> contained in <math id="S3.p7.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{V}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{V}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Abelian varieties associated to <math id="S4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SU(p,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SU(p,1)</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a simple algebraic group over <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> of hermitian type such that
<math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\mathbb{R})=\prod_{\alpha\in S}SU(p_{\alpha},q_{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\mathbb{R})=\prod_{\alpha\in S}SU(p_{\alpha},q_{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math>.
In his classification of equivariant holomorphic maps, Satake found that in addition to the standard representation (and its contragredient), there are nonstandard representations of <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-condition (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1. Kuga fiber varieties ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) if and only if one of <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> equals <math id="S4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.
We summarize his results for the groups of interest to us in the following theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 8</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Satake <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>*§3.2, pp. 447–449 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>*§8.3 (I<sup id="Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_sup"><span id="Thmtheorem8.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">′</span></sup>), pp. 268–269 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>*§IV.5, Exercise 1, p. 188)<span id="Thmtheorem8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-simple algebraic group of hermitian type such that each simple factor of <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to
<math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SU(p,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SU(p,1)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=p+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=p+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a totally real number field </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and an absolutely simple algebraic group </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{G}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> over </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that
</span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=\Res_{F/{\mathbb{Q}}}\widetilde{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Res</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=\Res_{F/{\mathbb{Q}}}\widetilde{G}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.
Let </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be the set of embeddings of </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> into </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.
Then </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\mathbb{R})=\prod_{\alpha\in S}G_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\mathbb{R})=\prod_{\alpha\in S}G_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, where
</span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\alpha}=\widetilde{G}\otimes_{F,\alpha}\mathbb{R}\cong SU(p,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\alpha}=\widetilde{G}\otimes_{F,\alpha}\mathbb{R}\cong SU(p,1)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, …, </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, there exists a homomorphism of </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-algebraic groups</span></p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}\colon G\to Sp(V_{k},\beta_{k})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}\colon G\to Sp(V_{k},\beta_{k}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a nondegenerate alternating form on a finite-dimensional
</span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-vector space </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{k}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> satisfies the </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-condition (</span><a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1. Kuga fiber varieties ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">), and, </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
is equivalent over </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> to
</span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bigoplus_{\alpha\in S}\left(\bigwedge^{k}\oplus\bigwedge^{m-k}\right)\circ P_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em">(</mo><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋀</mo><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">⊕</mo><msup><mo rspace="0em">⋀</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{\alpha\in S}\left(\bigwedge^{k}\oplus\bigwedge^{m-k}\right)\circ P_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,
where </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\alpha}\colon G(\mathbb{R})\to G_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\alpha}\colon G(\mathbb{R})\to G_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.2.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the projection.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is any symplectic representation of </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> which satisfies the </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-condition, then </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is equivalent to a direct sum </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{i=1}^{p}m_{i}\rho_{i}\oplus(\textup{triv})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>triv</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{i=1}^{p}m_{i}\rho_{i}\oplus(\textup{triv})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, where </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\textup{triv})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>triv</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\textup{triv})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> denotes a trivial representation.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 9</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We make several remarks about Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 8 (Satake []*§3.2, pp. 447–449 []*§8.3 (I′), pp. 268–269 []*§IV.5, Exercise 1, p. 188). ‣ 4. Abelian varieties associated to 
        
          
            ⁢
            S
            U
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                (
                p
                ,
                1
                ) ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and its proof.
Let <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{k}\to\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{k}\to\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Kuga fiber variety defined by
the symplectic representation (<a href="#S4.E1" title="In item 2 ‣ Theorem 8 (Satake []*§3.2, pp. 447–449 []*§8.3 (I′), pp. 268–269 []*§IV.5, Exercise 1, p. 188). ‣ 4. Abelian varieties associated to 
        
          
            ⁢
            S
            U
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                (
                p
                ,
                1
                ) ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>).</p>
<ol id="S4.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{1}\to\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{1}\to\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">pel</span>-family.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{m-k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{m-k}</annotation></semantics></math> are equivalent.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math> has no compact factors, it follows from
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>*Remark 3.5, p.  213 that each <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{k}\to\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{k}\to\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is a Hodge family.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> factors as</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\tilde{\rho}_{k}}}{{\longrightarrow}}G_{k}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\tilde{\varphi}_{k}}}{{\longrightarrow}}Sp(V_{k},\beta_{k})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mover><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mover><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mover><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\tilde{\rho}_{k}}}{{\longrightarrow}}G_{k}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\tilde{\varphi}_{k}}}{{\longrightarrow}}Sp(V_{k},\beta_{k}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I2.i4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{k}=\Res_{F/\mathbb{Q}}\widetilde{G}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Res</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{k}=\Res_{F/\mathbb{Q}}\widetilde{G}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for a semisimple algebraic group
<math id="S4.I2.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{G}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{G}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.I2.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.
We have <math id="S4.I2.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{k}(\mathbb{R})=\prod_{\alpha\in S}G_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{k}(\mathbb{R})=\prod_{\alpha\in S}G_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S4.I2.i4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{k,\alpha}\cong SU(p^{\prime},q^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{k,\alpha}\cong SU(p^{\prime},q^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here, <math id="S4.I2.i4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\prime}=\binom{p}{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\prime}=\binom{p}{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I2.i4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}=\binom{p}{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}=\binom{p}{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I2.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\rho}_{k,\mathbb{R}}\colon G(\mathbb{R})\to G_{k}(\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\rho}_{k,\mathbb{R}}\colon G(\mathbb{R})\to G_{k}(\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math> can be described as follows:
for <math id="S4.I2.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=(g_{\alpha})\in G(\mathbb{R})=\prod_{\alpha\in S}G_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=(g_{\alpha})\in G(\mathbb{R})=\prod_{\alpha\in S}G_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\rho}_{k,\mathbb{R}}(g)=\tilde{\rho}_{k,\alpha}(g_{\alpha})\in G_{k}(\mathbb{R})=\prod_{\alpha\in S}G_{k,\alpha}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\rho}_{k,\mathbb{R}}(g)=\tilde{\rho}_{k,\alpha}(g_{\alpha})\in G_{k}(\mathbb{R})=\prod_{\alpha\in S}G_{k,\alpha},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I2.i5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where
<math id="S4.I2.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\rho}_{k,\alpha}\colon G_{\alpha}=SU(p,1)\to G_{k,\alpha}=SU(p^{\prime},q^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\rho}_{k,\alpha}\colon G_{\alpha}=SU(p,1)\to G_{k,\alpha}=SU(p^{\prime},q^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>
is equivalent to the representation on the <math id="S4.I2.i5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th exterior power.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(6)</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By part <a href="#S4.I1.i2" title="item 2 ‣ Theorem 8 (Satake []*§3.2, pp. 447–449 []*§8.3 (I′), pp. 268–269 []*§IV.5, Exercise 1, p. 188). ‣ 4. Abelian varieties associated to 
        
          
            ⁢
            S
            U
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                (
                p
                ,
                1
                ) ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 8 (Satake []*§3.2, pp. 447–449 []*§8.3 (I′), pp. 268–269 []*§IV.5, Exercise 1, p. 188). ‣ 4. Abelian varieties associated to 
        
          
            ⁢
            S
            U
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                (
                p
                ,
                1
                ) ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, we can write</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{k,\mathbb{R}}=\bigoplus_{\alpha\in S}V_{k,\alpha}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{k,\mathbb{R}}=\bigoplus_{\alpha\in S}V_{k,\alpha},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I2.i6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.I2.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> acts trivially on <math id="S4.I2.i6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{k,\alpha^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{k,\alpha^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> unless <math id="S4.I2.i6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S4.I2.i6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\alpha,\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\alpha,\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> acts as <math id="S4.I2.i6.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bigwedge^{k}\oplus\bigwedge^{m-k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⋀</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">⊕</mo><mo>⋀</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigwedge^{k}\oplus\bigwedge^{m-k}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.I2.i6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{k,\alpha,\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{k,\alpha,\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(7)</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I2.i7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> acts trivially on <math id="S4.I2.i7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{k,\alpha^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{k,\alpha^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> unless <math id="S4.I2.i7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and,
the action of <math id="S4.I2.i7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{k,\alpha,\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{k,\alpha,\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.I2.i7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{k,\alpha,\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{k,\alpha,\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is equivalent to the direct sum of the standard representation and its contragredient.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(8)</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.I2.i8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be a general fiber of <math id="S4.I2.i8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{k}\to\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{k}\to\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S4.I2.i8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\neq\frac{m}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\neq\frac{m}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.I2.i8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is of type IV.
If <math id="S4.I2.i8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\frac{m}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\frac{m}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.I2.i8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is not of type IV, and both <math id="S4.I2.i8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I2.i8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(A_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(A_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> are semisimple.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 10</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be an abelian variety such that each simple factor of <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A)(\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A)(\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SU(p,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SU(p,1)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is dominated by abelian varieties.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Thanks to Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ 3. Semidomination ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, it suffices to show that <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is semidominated by some set of abelian varieties.
By Lemmas <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 1. ‣ 2.2. Two algebraic groups ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 6. ‣ 3. Semidomination ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we may assume that <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> is simple.
We are thus in the situation of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 8 (Satake []*§3.2, pp. 447–449 []*§8.3 (I′), pp. 268–269 []*§IV.5, Exercise 1, p. 188). ‣ 4. Abelian varieties associated to 
        
          
            ⁢
            S
            U
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                (
                p
                ,
                1
                ) ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> with <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=G^{\prime}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=G^{\prime}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Hodge family of <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> be such that <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{P}=A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{P}=A</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>, …, <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a Kuga fiber variety <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{k}\to\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{k}\to\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>
defined by the symplectic representation <math id="S4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S4.E1" title="In item 2 ‣ Theorem 8 (Satake []*§3.2, pp. 447–449 []*§8.3 (I′), pp. 268–269 []*§IV.5, Exercise 1, p. 188). ‣ 4. Abelian varieties associated to 
        
          
            ⁢
            S
            U
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                (
                p
                ,
                1
                ) ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>).
Let <math id="S4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{k}=(\mathcal{A}_{k})_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k}=(\mathcal{A}_{k})_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We will show that <math id="S4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is semidominated by the set</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=\left\{A_{1}^{n_{1}}\times\dots\times A_{p}^{n_{p}}\mid n_{i}\geq 0\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=\left\{A_{1}^{n_{1}}\times\dots\times A_{p}^{n_{p}}\mid n_{i}\geq 0\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume without loss of generality that <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> has no factors of CM-type.
Then the <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-representation defining <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> does not contain the trivial representation,
so by part <a href="#S4.I1.i3" title="item 3 ‣ Theorem 8 (Satake []*§3.2, pp. 447–449 []*§8.3 (I′), pp. 268–269 []*§IV.5, Exercise 1, p. 188). ‣ 4. Abelian varieties associated to 
        
          
            ⁢
            S
            U
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                (
                p
                ,
                1
                ) ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 8 (Satake []*§3.2, pp. 447–449 []*§8.3 (I′), pp. 268–269 []*§IV.5, Exercise 1, p. 188). ‣ 4. Abelian varieties associated to 
        
          
            ⁢
            S
            U
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                (
                p
                ,
                1
                ) ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, it is equivalent to <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1}\rho_{1}+\dots+m_{p}\rho_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1}\rho_{1}+\dots+m_{p}\rho_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>,
for some nonnegative integers <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1},\dots,m_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1},\dots,m_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is isogenous to <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{1}^{m_{1}}\times_{\mathcal{V}}\dots\times_{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{A}_{p}^{m_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{1}^{m_{1}}\times_{\mathcal{V}}\dots\times_{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{A}_{p}^{m_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It follows that <math id="S4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is isogenous to <math id="S4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1}^{m_{1}}\times\dots\times A_{p}^{m_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1}^{m_{1}}\times\dots\times A_{p}^{m_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
From now on we assume without loss of generality that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=A_{1}^{m_{1}}\times\dots\times A_{p}^{m_{p}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=A_{1}^{m_{1}}\times\dots\times A_{p}^{m_{p}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">and <math id="S4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, <math id="S4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We claim that <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A\times X)=G^{\prime}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A\times X)=G^{\prime}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.
More precisely, we mean that the projection <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A)\times G(X)\to G(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A)\times G(X)\to G(A)</annotation></semantics></math>, restricted to the subgroup
<math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A\times X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A\times X)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A)\times G(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A)\times G(X)</annotation></semantics></math> induces an isomorphism of <math id="S4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A\times X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A\times X)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>.
To see this, let <math id="S4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=A_{1}^{n_{1}}\times\dots\times A_{p}^{n_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=A_{1}^{n_{1}}\times\dots\times A_{p}^{n_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\times X=A_{1}^{m_{1}+n_{1}}\times A_{2}^{m_{2}+n_{2}}\times\dots\times A_{p}^{m_{p}+n_{p}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\times X=A_{1}^{m_{1}+n_{1}}\times A_{2}^{m_{2}+n_{2}}\times\dots\times A_{p}^{m_{p}+n_{p}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Thus <math id="S4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\times X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\times X</annotation></semantics></math> is the fiber over <math id="S4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> of the Kuga fiber variety</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{B}=\mathcal{A}_{1}^{m_{1}+n_{1}}\times_{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{A}_{2}^{m_{2}+n_{2}}\times_{\mathcal{V}}\dots\times_{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{A}_{p}^{m_{p}+n_{p}}\to\mathcal{V}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}=\mathcal{A}_{1}^{m_{1}+n_{1}}\times_{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{A}_{2}^{m_{2}+n_{2}}\times_{\mathcal{V}}\dots\times_{\mathcal{V}}\mathcal{A}_{p}^{m_{p}+n_{p}}\to\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">defined by the symplectic representation</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=(m_{1}+n_{1})\rho_{1}\oplus(m_{2}+n_{2})\rho_{2}\oplus\cdots\oplus(m_{p}+n_{p})\rho_{p}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=(m_{1}+n_{1})\rho_{1}\oplus(m_{2}+n_{2})\rho_{2}\oplus\cdots\oplus(m_{p}+n_{p})\rho_{p}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text">of <math id="S4.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>.
This is a Hodge family (see Remark <a href="#Thmtheorem9" title="Remark 9. ‣ 4. Abelian varieties associated to 
        
          
            ⁢
            S
            U
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                (
                p
                ,
                1
                ) ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.<a href="#S4.I2.i3" title="item 3 ‣ Remark 9. ‣ 4. Abelian varieties associated to 
        
          
            ⁢
            S
            U
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                (
                p
                ,
                1
                ) ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
Hence <math id="S4.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(\mathcal{B}_{Q})\subset G^{\prime}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(\mathcal{B}_{Q})\subset G^{\prime}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>.
But <math id="S4.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> is a quotient of <math id="S4.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A\times X)=G^{\prime}(\mathcal{B}_{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A\times X)=G^{\prime}(\mathcal{B}_{P})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore <math id="S4.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A\times X)=G^{\prime}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A\times X)=G^{\prime}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">We can write <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(A_{1})_{\mathbb{R}}=\prod_{\alpha\in S}L_{1,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℝ</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(A_{1})_{\mathbb{R}}=\prod_{\alpha\in S}L_{1,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1,\mathbb{R}}=\bigoplus_{\alpha\in S}V_{1,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1,\mathbb{R}}=\bigoplus_{\alpha\in S}V_{1,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> where
<math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1,\alpha}\cong U(p,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1,\alpha}\cong U(p,1)</annotation></semantics></math> acts trivially on <math id="S4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1,\alpha^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1,\alpha^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> unless
<math id="S4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and, <math id="S4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1,\alpha,\mathbb{C}}\cong GL_{m}(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1,\alpha,\mathbb{C}}\cong GL_{m}(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1,\alpha,\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1,\alpha,\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>
as the direct sum of the standard representation and its contragredient (Murty <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>).
As explained in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>*p. 351,
<math id="S4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1,\alpha,\mathbb{C}}=Y_{\alpha}\oplus\overline{Y}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1,\alpha,\mathbb{C}}=Y_{\alpha}\oplus\overline{Y}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and
its complex conjugate <math id="S4.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{Y}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{Y}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S4.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1,\alpha,\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1,\alpha,\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules,
<math id="S4.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL_{m}(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL_{m}(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S4.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> as the standard representation,
and on <math id="S4.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{Y}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{Y}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> as the contragredient.
<math id="S4.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is the direct sum of a <math id="S4.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional space of <math id="S4.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math>-forms and
a <math id="S4.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional space of <math id="S4.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-forms.
<math id="S4.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{Y}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{Y}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is the direct sum of a <math id="S4.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional space of <math id="S4.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math>-forms and a
<math id="S4.p6.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional space of <math id="S4.p6.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-forms.
Choose a basis <math id="S4.p6.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{u_{1},\dots,u_{m}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{u_{1},\dots,u_{m}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p6.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S4.p6.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, …, <math id="S4.p6.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S4.p6.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math>-forms and <math id="S4.p6.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S4.p6.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-form.
Then <math id="S4.p6.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{\overline{u}_{1},\dots,\overline{u}_{m}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\overline{u}_{1},\dots,\overline{u}_{m}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a basis of <math id="S4.p6.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{Y}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{Y}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">There is a natural action (from the left) of <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, the set of embeddings of <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\Aut(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\Aut(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in S</annotation></semantics></math> we have
<math id="S4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\left\{Y_{\alpha},\overline{Y}_{\alpha}\right\}=\left\{Y_{\sigma\alpha},\overline{Y}_{\sigma\alpha}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\left\{Y_{\alpha},\overline{Y}_{\alpha}\right\}=\left\{Y_{\sigma\alpha},\overline{Y}_{\sigma\alpha}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus the set <math id="S4.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{\alpha\in S}\left\{Y_{\alpha},\overline{Y}_{\alpha}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em">{</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{\alpha\in S}\left\{Y_{\alpha},\overline{Y}_{\alpha}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>
is invariant under the action of <math id="S4.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>, and every Galois conjugate of <math id="S4.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>
contains a nonzero <math id="S4.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math>-form.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, …, <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{m-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{m-1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the fundamental weights of <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SL_{m}(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SL_{m}(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the highest weight of the representation <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigwedge^{k}(\St)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>⋀</mo><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>St</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigwedge^{k}(\St)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\St)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>St</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\St)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes
the standard representation of <math id="S4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SL_{m}(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SL_{m}(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in S</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> be as in Remark <a href="#Thmtheorem9" title="Remark 9. ‣ 4. Abelian varieties associated to 
        
          
            ⁢
            S
            U
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                (
                p
                ,
                1
                ) ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.<a href="#S4.I2.i6" title="item 6 ‣ Remark 9. ‣ 4. Abelian varieties associated to 
        
          
            ⁢
            S
            U
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                (
                p
                ,
                1
                ) ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
Then <math id="S4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{k,\alpha,\mathbb{C}}=Y_{k,\alpha}\oplus\overline{Y}_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{k,\alpha,\mathbb{C}}=Y_{k,\alpha}\oplus\overline{Y}_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{k,\alpha}=\bigwedge^{k}Y_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msup><mo>⋀</mo><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{k,\alpha}=\bigwedge^{k}Y_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S4.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{Y}_{k,\alpha}=\bigwedge^{k}\overline{Y}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msup><mo>⋀</mo><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{Y}_{k,\alpha}=\bigwedge^{k}\overline{Y}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S4.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL_{m}(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL_{m}(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>-modules,
<math id="S4.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL_{m}(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL_{m}(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> acting on <math id="S4.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigwedge^{k}(\St)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>⋀</mo><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>St</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigwedge^{k}(\St)</annotation></semantics></math>, and on <math id="S4.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{Y}_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{Y}_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>
as the contragredient.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text"> is the direct sum of a <math id="S4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{p}{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{p}{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional space of <math id="S4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math>-forms and
a <math id="S4.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{p}{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{p}{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional space of <math id="S4.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-forms.
In particular, the highest weight vector in <math id="S4.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{k}=u_{1}\wedge\cdots\wedge u_{k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{k}=u_{1}\wedge\cdots\wedge u_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">which is a <math id="S4.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math>-form.
Define <math id="S4.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in GL_{m}(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in GL_{m}(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(u_{i})=u_{m+1-i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(u_{i})=u_{m+1-i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\prime}_{k}=\gamma(w_{k})=u_{m}\wedge\cdots\wedge u_{m+1-k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∧</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\prime}_{k}=\gamma(w_{k})=u_{m}\wedge\cdots\wedge u_{m+1-k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p10.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p10.3.1" class="ltx_text">is a <math id="S4.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-form.
<math id="S4.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{Y}_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{Y}_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is the direct sum of a <math id="S4.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{p}{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{p}{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional space of
<math id="S4.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math>-forms and a <math id="S4.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{p}{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{p}{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional space of <math id="S4.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-forms.
Observe that the set <math id="S4.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{\alpha\in S}\left\{Y_{k,\alpha},\overline{Y}_{k,\alpha}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em">{</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{\alpha\in S}\left\{Y_{k,\alpha},\overline{Y}_{k,\alpha}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>
is invariant under the action of <math id="S4.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>, so every Galois conjugate of <math id="S4.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>
contains a nonzero <math id="S4.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math>-form.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> be a positive integer.
Then <math id="S4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{j}Y_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{j}Y_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, the space of symmetric tensors on <math id="S4.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>,
is a representation of <math id="S4.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SL_{m}(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SL_{m}(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> with highest weight <math id="S4.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\mu_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\mu_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and highest weight vector <math id="S4.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w_{k})^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w_{k})^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S4.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{j}_{k,\alpha}\subset H^{j}(A_{k}^{j},\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{j}_{k,\alpha}\subset H^{j}(A_{k}^{j},\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S4.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SL_{m}(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SL_{m}(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>-module generated by <math id="S4.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w_{k})^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w_{k})^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The highest weight vector in <math id="S4.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{j}_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{j}_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w_{k})^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w_{k})^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> which is a <math id="S4.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j,0)</annotation></semantics></math>-form.
For <math id="S4.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbb{C}^{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>×</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbb{C}^{\times}</annotation></semantics></math>, the scalar matrix <math id="S4.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\cdot I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\cdot I</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S4.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{j}Y_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{j}Y_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> as multipliction by <math id="S4.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It follows that any <math id="S4.p11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SL_{m}(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SL_{m}(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>-submodule of <math id="S4.p11.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{j}Y_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{j}Y_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>,
in particular <math id="S4.p11.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{j}_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{j}_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, is a <math id="S4.p11.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL_{m}(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL_{m}(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>-submodule.
Therefore <math id="S4.p11.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{j}_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{j}_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the <math id="S4.p11.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,j)</annotation></semantics></math>-form <math id="S4.p11.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(w_{k})^{j}=(w^{\prime}_{k})^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(w_{k})^{j}=(w^{\prime}_{k})^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S4.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\Aut(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\Aut(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in S</annotation></semantics></math> we have
<math id="S4.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\{V^{j}_{k,\alpha},\overline{V}^{j}_{k,\alpha}\}=\{V^{j}_{k,\sigma\alpha},\overline{V}^{j}_{k,\sigma\alpha}\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\{V^{j}_{k,\alpha},\overline{V}^{j}_{k,\alpha}\}=\{V^{j}_{k,\sigma\alpha},\overline{V}^{j}_{k,\sigma\alpha}\}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Hence the set</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\{\,V^{j}_{k,\alpha}\mid\alpha\in S,\ 1\leq k\leq p,\ j&gt;0\,\right\}\cup\left\{\,\overline{V}^{j}_{k,\alpha}\mid\alpha\in S,\ 1\leq k\leq p,\ j&gt;0\,\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">{</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.170em">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.170em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\,V^{j}_{k,\alpha}\mid\alpha\in S,\ 1\leq k\leq p,\ j&gt;0\,\right\}\cup\left\{\,\overline{V}^{j}_{k,\alpha}\mid\alpha\in S,\ 1\leq k\leq p,\ j&gt;0\,\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">is invariant under the action of <math id="S4.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>, and every Galois conjugate of <math id="S4.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{j}_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{j}_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>
contains a nonzero <math id="S4.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j,0)</annotation></semantics></math>-form.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Any irreducible representation <math id="S4.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SL_{m}(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SL_{m}(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> has highest weight</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=a_{1}\mu_{1}+\dots+a_{p}\mu_{p}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=a_{1}\mu_{1}+\dots+a_{p}\mu_{p}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">where the <math id="S4.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are nonnegative integers.
Let <math id="S4.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{\mu}=\sum_{k=1}^{p}a_{k}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{\mu}=\sum_{k=1}^{p}a_{k}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Then the representation</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bigotimes_{k=1}^{p}V_{k,\alpha}^{a_{k}}\subset H^{a_{\mu}}(A_{1}^{a_{1}}\times\cdots\times A_{p}^{a_{p}},\mathbb{C})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigotimes_{k=1}^{p}V_{k,\alpha}^{a_{k}}\subset H^{a_{\mu}}(A_{1}^{a_{1}}\times\cdots\times A_{p}^{a_{p}},\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p13.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p13.3.1" class="ltx_text">has highest weight <math id="S4.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>.
The vector <math id="S4.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\mu}=\bigotimes_{k=1}^{p}(w_{k})^{a_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\mu}=\bigotimes_{k=1}^{p}(w_{k})^{a_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>
generates an irreducible submodule <math id="S4.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mu}^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi>α</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mu}^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> with highest weight <math id="S4.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that a scalar matrix <math id="S4.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\cdot I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\cdot I</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S4.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigotimes_{k=1}^{p}V_{k,\alpha}^{a_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨂</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigotimes_{k=1}^{p}V_{k,\alpha}^{a_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>
as multiplication by <math id="S4.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod_{k=1}^{p}z^{a_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{k=1}^{p}z^{a_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S4.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mu}^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi>α</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mu}^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S4.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL_{m}(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL_{m}(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>-module.
Hence <math id="S4.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mu}^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi>α</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mu}^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> contains both the <math id="S4.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{\mu},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{\mu},0)</annotation></semantics></math>-form <math id="S4.p13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> and
the <math id="S4.p13.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,a_{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,a_{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math>-form <math id="S4.p13.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{\prime}_{\mu}=\gamma(v_{\mu})=\bigotimes_{k=1}^{p}(w^{\prime}_{k})^{a_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{\prime}_{\mu}=\gamma(v_{\mu})=\bigotimes_{k=1}^{p}(w^{\prime}_{k})^{a_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S4.p13.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\Aut(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\Aut(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>, we have
<math id="S4.p13.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(V_{\mu}^{\alpha})\in\{V_{\mu}^{\sigma\alpha},V_{\overline{\mu}}^{\sigma\alpha}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(V_{\mu}^{\alpha})\in\{V_{\mu}^{\sigma\alpha},V_{\overline{\mu}}^{\sigma\alpha}\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S4.p13.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mu}=a_{p}\mu_{1}+\dots+a_{1}\mu_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mu}=a_{p}\mu_{1}+\dots+a_{1}\mu_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus the set</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bigg\{\,V_{\mu}^{\alpha}\ \big|\ \alpha\in S,\ \mu=\sum_{i=1}^{p}a_{i}\mu_{i},a_{i}\geq 0\,\bigg\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></munderover><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigg\{\,V_{\mu}^{\alpha}\ \big|\ \alpha\in S,\ \mu=\sum_{i=1}^{p}a_{i}\mu_{i},a_{i}\geq 0\,\bigg\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p13.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p13.4.1" class="ltx_text">is invariant under the action of <math id="S4.p13.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>, and every Galois conjugate of
<math id="S4.p13.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mu}^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi>α</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mu}^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a nonzero <math id="S4.p13.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{\mu},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{\mu},0)</annotation></semantics></math>-form.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Any irreducible representation <math id="S4.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A)_{\mathbb{C}}=\prod_{\alpha\in S}G^{\prime}(A)_{\alpha,\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A)_{\mathbb{C}}=\prod_{\alpha\in S}G^{\prime}(A)_{\alpha,\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is of the form <math id="S4.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\bigotimes_{\alpha\in S}\pi_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⨂</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\bigotimes_{\alpha\in S}\pi_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>
is an irreducible representation of <math id="S4.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A)_{\alpha,\mathbb{C}}\cong SL_{m}(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A)_{\alpha,\mathbb{C}}\cong SL_{m}(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\rho}=\bigotimes_{\alpha\in S}V_{\mu_{\alpha}}^{\alpha}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mi>α</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\rho}=\bigotimes_{\alpha\in S}V_{\mu_{\alpha}}^{\alpha},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p14.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p14.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S4.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is the highest weight of <math id="S4.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S4.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> is an irreducible <math id="S4.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A)_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A)_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>-submodule of <math id="S4.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{c}(A_{\rho},\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{c}(A_{\rho},\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>,
for some <math id="S4.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\rho}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\rho}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and some positive integer <math id="S4.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, on which <math id="S4.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A)_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A)_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> acts as <math id="S4.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since every Galois conjugate of each <math id="S4.p14.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mu_{\alpha}}^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mi>α</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mu_{\alpha}}^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>
contains a nonzero <math id="S4.p14.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{\mu_{\alpha}},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{\mu_{\alpha}},0)</annotation></semantics></math> form,
it follows that every Galois conjugate of <math id="S4.p14.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a nonzero <math id="S4.p14.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,0)</annotation></semantics></math>-form.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">To complete the proof of the theorem we need to show that
the <math id="S4.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(A_{\rho})_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(A_{\rho})_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S4.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> is actually a <math id="S4.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A_{\rho})_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A_{\rho})_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>-module.
By Proposition <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 2. ‣ 2.2. Two algebraic groups ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, it is sufficient to show that
<math id="S4.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S4.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\big(L(A_{\rho})\big)_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\big(L(A_{\rho})\big)_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>-module.
Since <math id="S4.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{m-k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{m-k}</annotation></semantics></math> are isomorphic (Remark <a href="#Thmtheorem9" title="Remark 9. ‣ 4. Abelian varieties associated to 
        
          
            ⁢
            S
            U
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                (
                p
                ,
                1
                ) ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.<a href="#S4.I2.i2" title="item 2 ‣ Remark 9. ‣ 4. Abelian varieties associated to 
        
          
            ⁢
            S
            U
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                (
                p
                ,
                1
                ) ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), we may write</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\rho}=\prod_{2k\leq m}A_{k}^{n_{k}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\rho}=\prod_{2k\leq m}A_{k}^{n_{k}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p15.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p15.2.1" class="ltx_text">Assume, without loss of generality, that each <math id="S4.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{k}&gt;0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{k}&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math> Then</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(A_{\rho})=\prod_{2k\leq m}L(A_{k})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(A_{\rho})=\prod_{2k\leq m}L(A_{k}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p15.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p15.3.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S4.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\frac{m}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\frac{m}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(A_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(A_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(A_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(A_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> are semisimple (Remark <a href="#Thmtheorem9" title="Remark 9. ‣ 4. Abelian varieties associated to 
        
          
            ⁢
            S
            U
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                (
                p
                ,
                1
                ) ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.<a href="#S4.I2.i8" title="item 8 ‣ Remark 9. ‣ 4. Abelian varieties associated to 
        
          
            ⁢
            S
            U
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                (
                p
                ,
                1
                ) ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), so it is sufficient to show that
<math id="S4.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S4.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>-module, where</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=\prod_{1\leq k&lt;\frac{m}{2}}Z\big(L(A_{k})\big)_{\mathbb{C}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=\prod_{1\leq k&lt;\frac{m}{2}}Z\big(L(A_{k})\big)_{\mathbb{C}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p15.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p15.4.1" class="ltx_text">Now, for <math id="S4.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\neq\frac{m}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\neq\frac{m}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\big(L(A_{k})\big)_{\mathbb{C}}=\prod_{\alpha\in S}Z_{k,\alpha}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\big(L(A_{k})\big)_{\mathbb{C}}=\prod_{\alpha\in S}Z_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p15.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p15.5.1" class="ltx_text">where each <math id="S4.p15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{k,\alpha}\cong\mathbb{C}^{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>×</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{k,\alpha}\cong\mathbb{C}^{\times}</annotation></semantics></math>, and in the decomposition (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In item 6 ‣ Remark 9. ‣ 4. Abelian varieties associated to 
        
          
            ⁢
            S
            U
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                (
                p
                ,
                1
                ) ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>),
<math id="S4.p15.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> acts trivially on <math id="S4.p15.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{k,\alpha^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{k,\alpha^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> unless <math id="S4.p15.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A scalar <math id="S4.p15.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\alpha}\in Z_{k.\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\alpha}\in Z_{k.\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S4.p15.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> as multiplication by <math id="S4.p15.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and on <math id="S4.p15.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{Y}_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{Y}_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> as multiplication by <math id="S4.p15.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{z}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{z}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>;
thus <math id="S4.p15.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p15.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{Y}_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{Y}_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> are both <math id="S4.p15.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules.
The scalar <math id="S4.p15.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> acts as multiplication by <math id="S4.p15.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{j}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{j}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p15.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{j}_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{j}_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>;
so <math id="S4.p15.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{j}_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{j}_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S4.p15.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{k,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{k,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>-module.
Now let</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=(z_{k,\alpha})\in Z\big(L(A_{\rho})\big)_{\mathbb{C}}=\prod_{1\leq k&lt;\frac{m}{2}}\ \prod_{\alpha\in S}Z_{k,\alpha}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=(z_{k,\alpha})\in Z\big(L(A_{\rho})\big)_{\mathbb{C}}=\prod_{1\leq k&lt;\frac{m}{2}}\ \prod_{\alpha\in S}Z_{k,\alpha}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p15.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p15.6.1" class="ltx_text">For a given <math id="S4.p15.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in S</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p15.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S4.p15.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigotimes_{k=1}^{p}V_{k,\alpha}^{a_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨂</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigotimes_{k=1}^{p}V_{k,\alpha}^{a_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> as multiplication by
<math id="S4.p15.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod_{2k&lt;m}z_{k,\alpha}^{a_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{2k&lt;m}z_{k,\alpha}^{a_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence any subspace of it (in particular, <math id="S4.p15.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\alpha}_{\mu_{\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mi>α</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\alpha}_{\mu_{\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math>) is a
<math id="S4.p15.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>-module.
It follows that <math id="S4.p15.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\rho}=\bigotimes_{\alpha\in S}V_{\mu_{\alpha}}^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⨂</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mi>α</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\rho}=\bigotimes_{\alpha\in S}V_{\mu_{\alpha}}^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is a
<math id="S4.p15.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>-module.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 11</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The assumption that <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 10. ‣ 4. Abelian varieties associated to 
        
          
            ⁢
            S
            U
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                (
                p
                ,
                1
                ) ‣ Hodge structures associated to 
      
        
          ⁢
          S
          U
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              (
              p
              ,
              1
              )" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> is only for convenience.
If <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then a result of Shimura <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>*Proposition 14, p. 176
implies that <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is of CM-type, so the theorem is a special case of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>*Theorem 4, p. 159.
If <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SU(p,1)\cong Sp_{2}(\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SU(p,1)\cong Sp_{2}(\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math>, so the theorem is a special case of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>*Theorem 5.1, p. 348.</p>
</div>
</div>
